WEBVTT - The Sound Above: Episode 1 (The First Room: Sergio Pereira & Chicago Alt.NET Monologue)
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.000
<v Mike Hall>Welcome to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 1 of our technical rewatch series.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:22.000
<v Mike Hall>Today we are going back to November 19, 2011, on-site at Software Craftsmanship North America at the Swissotel in downtown Chicago.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:32.000
<v Mike Hall>My very first guest when we launched the UGtastic interview series was Sergio Pereira, the organizer of Chicago Alt.NET.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.000
<v Mike Hall>There is a reason I asked Sergio to sit down first. Chicago Alt.NET was not the first user group I ever walked into or attended. It was the first user group where I ever stood up and gave a presentation.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:54.000
<v Mike Hall>To understand why that room mattered, you have to remember what enterprise .NET felt like in 2007 and 2008. Most corporate shops treated Microsoft's default box products as the law.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:05.000
<v Mike Hall>If a tool or workflow did not ship inside Visual Studio or MSDN, you were told not to touch it. On April 27, 2007, David Laribee published the Alt.NET Manifesto.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:14.000
<v Mike Hall>He set out four core tenets for developers who refused to accept the default corporate box.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.000
<v Mike Hall>First: You are the type of developer who uses what works while keeping an eye out for a better way.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.000
<v Mike Hall>Second: You reach outside the mainstream to adopt the best of any community: Open Source, Agile, Java, Ruby, and beyond.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.000
<v Mike Hall>Third: You are not content with the status quo. Things can always be better expressed, more elegant, simpler, more mutable, and higher quality.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
<v Mike Hall>Fourth: You know tools are great, but they only take you so far. It is the principles and knowledge that really matter.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.000
<v Mike Hall>That October, developers gathered in Austin for the first Alt.NET Open Spaces, which Martin Fowler documented in his Bliki.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:08.000
<v Mike Hall>Sergio Pereira brought that exact spirit to Chicago. On the second Wednesday of every month at 6:30 PM, right when commuters could catch the Metra back to the suburbs, about 25 of us took the elevator up to Redpoint Technologies on the 84th floor of the Sears Tower.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:25.000
<v Mike Hall>On November 12, 2008, I stood up in that room and gave my first presentation on NHaml alongside Adam Tybor presenting on the Spark view engine.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:37.000
<v Mike Hall>Here is the question that guides our rewatch today: How do working engineers build practical autonomy inside corporate environments without waiting for vendor permission?

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:51.000
<v Mike Hall>When ASP.NET MVC was released, it marked one of the biggest strategic turns in Microsoft history. For nearly a decade, Redmond had insisted on WebForms, complex page lifecycles, and viewstate, hiding the raw HTTP protocol behind corporate-approved controls.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:04.000
<v Mike Hall>The release of ASP.NET MVC was the direct victory of the Ruby community. Frameworks like Rails, Sinatra, and Merb had proved to the entire industry that convention over configuration, lightweight routing, and testable controllers were vastly superior for web development.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:19.000
<v Mike Hall>Microsoft's pivot to MVC was a watershed moment: it proved that when developers look outside the approved corporate checklist and insist on better architecture, even the largest software vendor in the world has to adapt.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:32.000
<v Mike Hall>Alt.NET was never about hating .NET. As Sergio says right at the start of this tape, we were developers who liked .NET, used it every day, and wanted to bring continuous improvement, automated testing, NHibernate, StructureMap, and IronRuby into our daily work.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:45.000
<v Mike Hall>Let us roll the 2011 hallway conversation with Sergio Pereira. Listen to how Sergio describes the charter of the group: not bringing in polished marketing speakers, but encouraging regular team developers to take the talks they gave at lunch with their coworkers and share them with a room of peers who wanted to see a better way to build software.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:58.000
<v Mike Hall>How does this connect to where we sit in 2026? In 2008, the default vendor trap was drag-and-drop designer wizards that hid coupling and made unit testing impossible.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:10.000
<v Mike Hall>Today, the default vendor trap is black-box cloud AI generation that produces thousands of lines of unverified code while hiding the domain boundaries underneath.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:22.000
<v Mike Hall>The lesson from Chicago Alt.NET is unchanged: tools are instruments, not religions. You do not accept a workflow just because a vendor ships it as the default.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:35.000
<v Mike Hall>You keep your domain models clean, you run your own fast local verification loops, and you judge every tool by whether it holds up under test.

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:47.000
<v Mike Hall>Thank you for joining Episode 1 of The Sound Above. You can read the full interactive transcript with Sergio Pereira, along with links to David Laribee's 2007 Manifesto, Martin Fowler's Austin notes, Scott Guthrie's early MVC previews, and Jeremy Miller's retrospectives, right on the Episode 1 page at just3ws.com.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:05:00.000
<v Mike Hall>To help support our independent compute and archive preservation, visit patreon.com/whoistechcmty.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:15.000
<v Mike Hall>In Episode 2, we sit down with Ray Hightower of ChicagoRuby to talk about grassroots hospitality and opening doors across communities. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.
